{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SCjQDKciyuzcJRPKTsmGSc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Found important differences:\n\n**R10.1 (-u uniform spacing)**: Our binary does NOT implement `-u` at all! \n- `gfmt -u` on \"word1    word2     word3      word4\" → \"word1 word2 word3 word4\" (spaces collapsed)\n- Our binary → \"word1    word2     word3      word4\" (spaces preserved as-is, line fits in width so not reformatted)\n\nThe issue is in `formatParagraph`: when a line is short enough (fits within width), it's returned as-is:\n\n\nThe early return for short single-line paragraphs skips all formatting, including space normalization. With `-u` enabled, we need to normalize spacing even when the line doesn't need reformatting. The problem is that `extractWords` and `fillLines` handle normalization for text that gets reformatted, but short lines that bypass this path never get their spaces normalized. when they don't need wrapping. The `-t` flag for tagged paragraphs is working correctly already. Now looking at the `-p` prefix flag, there's an issue with how indented prefix lines are handled. When a line has leading whitespace before the prefix character, our implementation strips that whitespace but gfmt preserves it. The `stripPrefix` function is trimming spaces and tabs before checking for the prefix, which loses the original indentation that should be kept. I see the issue now—`stripPrefix` removes the leading whitespace and the prefix itself, returning just the content, but then `writePrefixBlock` re-adds only the prefix without preserving those original leading spaces. So a line like \"  >Indented prefix line.\" becomes \">Indented prefix line.\" instead of keeping the indentation. I need to modify `stripPrefix` to track and return the original leading whitespace so it can be restored when writing the block back out.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_detai