{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Xtezt2yrzCkpaX36wk7o6L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see the differences clearly:\n\n**R10.1 (-u uniform spacing)**:\n1. Short line \"a   b   c\" with `-u`: gfmt normalizes to \"a b c\", our binary leaves as \"a   b   c\"\n2. Long line that wraps with `-u`: both produce same output (because our code does normalize when wrapping)\n3. Without `-u`, \"a   b   c\" is preserved as-is by both (correct)\n\nThe issue is that with `-u`, our code needs to normalize spacing even for short lines that don't need wrapping. The early return in `formatParagraph` at line 274 (`if len(lines) == 1 && len(lines[0]) <= flagWidth`) bypasses normalization.\n\n\n**R11.1 (-p prefix)**:\n1. \"  >text here\" with `-p '>'`: gfmt produces \"  >text here\" (preserves leading whitespace), our binary produces \">text here\" (strips it)\n\nThe fix needed: when we strip the prefix, we need to also remember and re-add any leading whitespace.\n\n**R12.1 (-t tagged paragraph)**: All test cases matched. The existing implementation is correct.\n\nNow I'm implementing the fixes. For R10.1, I need to normalize spacing even for short lines when uniform spacing is enabled. For R11.1, I need to preserve any leading whitespace that appears before the prefix when stripping it. The key insight is that the difference between `-u` and the default only shows up for short lines that skip reformatting. When `-u` is set, I need to always process lines through word extraction and filling, even if they're short enough to fit as-is. Let me update `formatParagraph` to handle this by removing the early return when the uniform flag is active.\n\nFor the prefix whitespace issue, I need to modify how `stripPrefix` works to preserve any leading whitespace before the prefix marker, then restore it when writing the output in `writePrefixBlock`. the group is formatted and each output line gets the prefix re-added. So I need to track the leading whitespace separately for each line in the group, then restore it when writing the formatted output.\n\nLet me test how gfmt actually handles multiple prefixed lines with varying indentation to understand the expected behavior.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":562,"cache_read_input_tokens":42211,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":562},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8b01509-4122-49cf-a490-71fd22416bc5","uuid":"4292d0e1-b74c-4c62-b46b-0337549fdc10"}
