{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GtGhg7HMGCaKqHEAWAMFoZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I can see it clearly!\n\nFor the long line \"End. Start...\" with -u vs without -u, the output is identical — both have single space after \"End.\" and single space everywhere else. This confirms that gfmt's default wrapping already normalizes to single space, and \"End.\" followed by \"Start\" uses single space because gfmt doesn't consider \"End.\" as a sentence ending in this context? Or does it?\n\nWait, looking at the second test case:\n\nWith -u: \"This is a test.  Next sentence. Another one!  Wow? Really.  Yes indeed\"\n\n\nWithout -u: \"This is a test.   Next sentence. Another one!  Wow? Really.     Yes indeed\"\n\nSo the pattern is becoming clearer — with -u, gfmt normalizes spacing after sentence-ending punctuation to double space, but it's inconsistent about what it considers a sentence ending. \"sentence.\" gets single space before \"Another\" even with -u, while \"test.\" gets double space before \"Next\". Without -u, the original spacing is mostly preserved, though some normalization still happens.\n\nI'm wondering if gfmt's sentence detection requires more than just punctuation and capitalization — maybe it's looking at word length, context, or some other heuristic that makes \"sentence.\" not register as a true sentence ending in this particular case.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1693,"cache_read_input_tokens":49590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1693},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8b01509-4122-49cf-a490-71fd22416bc5","uuid":"56108fa3-ff8a-4491-a24a-162dbae2fa7a"}
